"It was my second day on the job and the second time I was asked that question. So, I guess I should begin with a story about a product that’s become pretty familiar in the Nigerian market today.\n\nOn December 31st, 2020, Femi Aluko tested positive for COVID. The result came late at night and he couldn’t travel back home. So he tried to order food that evening, only to realize the process was far from seamless.\n\nHe found there was an execution gap and set out to build a product capable of delivering meals in under 30 minutes.\n\nAnd today, we have Chowdeck. A food delivery product.\n\nThe restaurant gets your order\n\nThe rider delivers it\n\nThe designer designed the app\n\nThe engineers built it\n\nCustomer support helps if something goes wrong\n\nThe marketer convinced you to download it.\n\nSo, what exactly did the product manager do?\n\nNothing obvious. And that’s exactly why people keep asking.\n\nWe tend to value visible work.\nHumans naturally reward what they can see.\n\nA logo. A website. A contract. A marketing campaign.\n\nPeople know who created them and so, they don’t ask the essence of these roles.\n\nBut with PMs, the work is harder to point at.\n\nThese popular departments are hired to create. PMs? They are hired to prevent.\n\nPrevent confusion, prevent wastage, prevent failure, prevent losses, prevent teams from building the wrong thing, prevent egos from their “million-dollar idea.” The kind where if you do it well enough, people assume there was never a problem to begin with.\n\nBut to accomplish all of this, they have to know how to decide. Everything else; the roadmaps, the morning calls, the documents with names like PRD that nobody outside tech has heard of, is paperwork wrapped around that one thing.\n\nDecision-making!\n\nUsers tend to notice products when something goes wrong. You may never hear, “Wow, what brilliant prioritisation,” but instead, “This app is rubbish.” PMs succeed by knowing the right way to deal with such feedback.\n\nProduct management isn’t about ideas.\nSpeaking of a million-dollar idea, everyone has them. And AI has made it so much easier for us to act on them.\n\nBut execution is choosing! Choosing the right one. Apple says no to hundreds of features.\n\nGoogle kills products.\n\nNetflix removes shows.\n\nMeta copies features.\n\nEvery product is mostly decisions about what not to do.\n\nWhen Femi Aluko found there was an execution gap in food delivery, that was him doing product thinking. But not many founders are skilled at it.\n\nThat problem you’re setting out to solve, is it a general one, niche, or just a “you” problem? Do people care enough to actually pay for it? This is what PMs are here for.\n\nWhy did product management even become a thing?\nFactories didn’t exactly need PMs.\n\nSoftware changed everything. PMs became more common as software became too complex for the CEO to understand every part.\n\nMany startups don’t hire PMs until teams become too large for founders to coordinate directly.\n\nMany startups hire PMs because they understand resources are finite. So yes, this is also economics.\n\nEvery company has finite money, engineers, designers, time, attention. If you can only build five things, choosing what not to build becomes more important than building everything.\n\nIt’s called Opportunity Cost, and it’s one of the most important ideas in product management.\n\nEvery “yes” silently says “no” to something else.\n\nWhat it looks like on a random Tuesday\nMostly, it looks like your data isn’t giving you all the answers.\n\nYou see, with Chowdeck; on screen, a pickup can look perfectly smooth. But in real life, the rider is standing there for 11 minutes because the food isn’t bagged and nobody at the counter knows who’s meant to hand it over.\n\nAnd so, they worked to bridge that gap by acquiring Mira, a restaurant Point of Sale (POS) and software company, to directly integrate with the restaurant’s internal kitchen backend.\n\nThat solution is what the PM might’ve stayed awake to find. Could take weeks, could take months. But figuring out why a specific flow fails in the real world? That starts on a random Tuesday, digging through user analytics that don’t quite tell you what you need to know.\n\nSometime ago, they found that Google Maps wasn’t accurate enough for Lagos, so they built their own maps. Which tells you the other half of the job: discovering that the tool the rest of the world uses may not survive contact with here.\n\nNow, bringing this a little closer to my world, I’m currently leading two teams across EdTech and AgriTech. One of which is non-profit, both of which are powered by AI. Okay, is it even a 2026 product if there isn’t some AI integration? LOL.\n\nBut the designer has lost sleep trying to get the flow right. The engineers are deep in the code base, wrestling bugs and hitting blockers. Months of sweat have been put into this. And now, with beta testers, the iterations worth making are evident.\n\nBut as I sit through another one of our meetings, I prepare to do the part I know they dread. Staring at the screen before me, I finally say it, “I recommend reducing the homepage tabs from three to one. It creates decision fatigue for the user.”\n\nBooks may never tell you, but sometimes your job is to kill good work. Not because the design was bad, not because engineering failed, or that effort wasn’t put into it, but because users only care about what’s useful to them with the least possible stress exerted.\n\nSo, as I picked apart the choices made on each screen, I could tell the unspoken question their eyes were asking. “What do product managers even do?”\n\nThis! All of this, is your answer."
